The National Australia Bank has forecast another rate hike to 4.35 per cent in November. Elsewhere, Wall Street closed out its worst month and quarter of the year with more losses on Friday. The S&P 500 slipped 0.3 per cent and the majority of stocks within the index sank. The Dow Jones fell 0.5 per cent and the Nasdaq composite edged 0.1 per cent higher. The Australian sharemarket is set to retreat, with futures pointing to a fall of 38 points, or 0.5 per cent, at the open. Solid gains for stocks early on faded as pressure built from within the bond market. After easing earlier in the day on encouraging signals about inflation, Treasury yields got back to rising as the day progressed. The yield on the 10-year Treasury yield returned to 4.58 per cent, where it was late Thursday, after dipping to 4.52 per cent. It’s again near its highest level since 2007.","entities":["Australian sharemarket","Dow Jones","National Australia Bank","rate hike to 4.35 per cent in November","ASX set to open lower as Wall Street closes September with more losses","The Sydney Morning Herald","bond market","10-year Treasury yield","S&P 500","Nasdaq composite","Treasury yields","Wall Street","inflation"],"facts":["ASX to open lower; Wall Street falls as the clamp tightens from rising bond yields -MENTIONS-> Australian sharemarket","ASX set to drop as Wall Street’s September slump deepens -MENTIONS-> Australian sharemarket","ASX rises after Wall Street gains on rate optimism; Oil drops -MENTIONS-> Australian sharemarket","Gary Glover -SAID-> Australian sharemarket","Ben Clark -SAID-> Australian sharemarket"]}
